# A2A Your First Agent

This tutorial builds one Ash resource, projects its public actions into A2A
skills, and talks to the result three ways: a direct dispatch, a supervised
`AshA2A.Agent` process, and the agent card. By the end you will have sent a
message to a real agent and gotten a real task back.

Every code cell runs as-is: the setup cell installs `ash` and `ash_a2a`, so
this livebook needs no Mix project behind it.

## Setup

```elixir
Mix.install([{:ash, "~> 3.0"}, {:ash_a2a, "~> 26.9.31"}],
  consolidate_protocols: false,
  config: [
    ash: [
      validate_domain_resource_inclusion?: false,
      validate_domain_config_inclusion?: false
    ]
  ]
)
```

## Define the resource, domain, and agent

An `AshA2A` resource is a normal Ash resource. Its *public actions* are the
capability surface: public actions need no `a2a` declaration at all. The
optional `a2a do skill/2` block only overrides display metadata and routing
names; it cannot manufacture a capability for an action that doesn't exist.

```elixir
defmodule Tutorial.Todo do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Tutorial.Domain,
    data_layer: Ash.DataLayer.Ets,
    extensions: [AshA2A]

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key(:id)
    attribute(:title, :string, allow_nil?: false, public?: true)
    attribute(:done, :boolean, default: false, public?: true)
  end

  actions do
    defaults([:read, :create, :update, :destroy])
  end

  a2a do
    skill(:create_todo, :create, description: "Create a new todo")
    skill(:list_todos, :read)
  end
end
```

```elixir
defmodule Tutorial.Domain do
  use Ash.Domain, extensions: [AshA2A]

  resources do
    resource(Tutorial.Todo)
  end
end

defmodule Tutorial.TodoAgent do
  use AshA2A.Agent, resource_or_domain: Tutorial.Domain, name: "tutorial_todos"
end
```

## Inspect the compiled capability index

Compilation projects the public actions into a capability index. Dispatch
resolves skills by full id (`"Tutorial.Todo.create"`) or display name
(`:create_todo`); a resource or domain exposing exactly one skill routes
implicitly, two or more require `metadata["skill"]` on the message.

```elixir
AshA2A.Info.capability_index!(Tutorial.Domain)
|> Enum.map(&{&1.id, &1.name, &1.action, &1.consequence})
#=> [
#=>   {"Tutorial.Todo.create", :create_todo, :create, :change},
#=>   {"Tutorial.Todo.read", :list_todos, :read, :observe},
#=>   {"Tutorial.Todo.update", :update, :update, :change},
#=>   {"Tutorial.Todo.destroy", :destroy, :destroy, :change}
#=> ]
```

The `:consequence` classification decides how each skill is served: `:observe`
skills are plain reads, while `:change` skills route through the receipted
`AshA2A.CommandBus` when served by an agent (authority comes in
[Secure Your Agent](secure-your-agent.livemd)).

## Direct dispatch (no process)

Dispatch an inbound `AshA2A.Protocol.Message` straight to the domain — no
agent process required. The message's Data part becomes the action input;
with more than one skill on the card, the skill goes in `metadata["skill"]`.
Here we dispatch the `:observe` skill, which is served straight from the
capability index with no authority wiring.

```elixir
list_msg =
  AshA2A.Protocol.Message.new_user([AshA2A.Protocol.Part.Data.new(%{})])
  |> struct!(metadata: %{"skill" => "list_todos"})

{:reply, [data_part]} =
  AshA2A.Dispatcher.dispatch("list_todos", list_msg, Tutorial.Domain)

data_part.data
#=> %{"results" => []}
```

## Call the agent

A bare `dispatch/6` call is synchronous and process-free. To run the same
resource as a long-lived, addressable agent, `use AshA2A.Agent` and start it
directly. In an OTP app you would instead register it with
`config :ash_a2a, :agents, [Tutorial.TodoAgent]` and let the `ash_a2a`
application supervise it.

```elixir
{:ok, _pid} = Tutorial.TodoAgent.start_link([])

create_msg =
  AshA2A.Protocol.Message.new_user([AshA2A.Protocol.Part.Data.new(%{"title" => "Write livebooks"})])
  |> struct!(metadata: %{"skill" => "create_todo"})

{:ok, task} = AshA2A.Protocol.call(Tutorial.TodoAgent, create_msg)
task.status.state
#=> :completed
```

## Read the task back and read history

Every turn is a real `AshA2A.Protocol.Task` with its own id. A text-only
message (no Data part) dispatches with an empty input map — handy for
skills whose action needs no arguments.

```elixir
text_msg =
  AshA2A.Protocol.Message.new_user("list my todos")
  |> struct!(metadata: %{"skill" => "list_todos"})

{:ok, task2} = AshA2A.Protocol.call(Tutorial.TodoAgent, text_msg)
:completed = task2.status.state

hd(task2.artifacts).parts
|> hd()
|> then(& &1.data["results"])
|> Enum.map(& &1["title"])
#=> ["Write livebooks"]
```

## The agent card

The running agent's card is built from the exact same verified capability
index dispatch serves, so the card can never advertise a skill dispatch
can't actually run.

```elixir
card = AshA2A.Info.agent_card(Tutorial.Domain)
card.name
#=> "tutorial_todos"

Enum.map(card.skills, & &1.id)
#=> ["Tutorial.Todo.create", "Tutorial.Todo.read",
#=>  "Tutorial.Todo.update", "Tutorial.Todo.destroy"]
```

## What you built, and what's next

You now have: a real Ash resource whose public actions are projected into a
verified capability index, a working direct dispatch, and a supervised
`AshA2A.Agent` process whose card is derived from that same index.

From here:

- authenticate callers and grant per-capability authority in
  [Secure Your Agent](secure-your-agent.livemd);
- stream large reads lazily and resolve `:input_required` pauses in
  [Stream and Elicit](stream-and-elicit.livemd);
- serve the agent over HTTP (JSON-RPC, SSE streaming, `AshA2A.Protocol.Client`)
  with the plain-markdown
  [Getting Started](../docs/tutorials/getting-started.md) tutorial.
